## Q4, can a filer score stand in for the NVD

{% set q4 = figure("backlog.q4") %}
{{ q4.subtitle }}.

On {{ v("backlog.q4.cves") }} CVEs scored with CVSS 3.1 by both their filer and the NVD, the two agree
well on the attack vector (kappa {{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_av") }}, {{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_av_low") }} to
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_av_high") }}) and poorly on attack complexity (kappa
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_ac") }}). The three impact metrics fall between, at kappa
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_c") }} for confidentiality, {{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_i") }} for integrity and
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_a") }} for availability. On the severity band the weighted kappa is
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_band") }}, and filers score lower than the NVD on average
({{ v("backlog.q4.direction_band") }} on the band). A filer score can stand in for the NVD on the
attack vector, much less on attack complexity, and only in part on the impact.

{{ table("backlog.q4.by_filer_type") }}

The data lead would push back: a filer has reasons to score its own product low, and the NVD's
score is also a label made by people, so the kappa measures agreement between two labels, neither
of them the true severity.

